# Structural analysis

## A. Maximum subdivision length

[DIRECTLY FROM DATA]

Maximum subdivision length is **not sufficient to determine \(b(T)\)**.

At \(S=6\):

* \((6,0,\ldots,0)\): \(b(T)=3\);
* \((2,2,2,0,\ldots,0)\): \(b(T)=4\).

So both maximum subdivision lengths 6 and 2 occur at the same total \(S\), with different burning numbers.

For \(S=7,\ldots,10\), the overlap is even stronger: \(b(T)=4\) occurs for both very large maximum lengths and relatively small ones.

For example, at \(S=10\):

$$
(10,0,\ldots,0)
$$

has \(b(T)=4\), while

$$
(2,2,2,2,2,0,\ldots,0)
$$

also has \(b(T)=4\).

[STRUCTURAL HYPOTHESIS]

Maximum arm length by itself is therefore unlikely to be the only useful structural descriptor.

---

## B. Number of subdivided edges

[DIRECTLY FROM DATA]

The number of affected edges is also not sufficient.

At \(S=6\), all three patterns

$$
(4,1,1),\quad(3,2,1),\quad(2,2,2)
$$

have exactly three subdivided edges, but only

$$
(2,2,2)
$$

has \(b(T)=4\).

At larger \(S\), the same number of subdivided edges can also correspond to both \(b=3\) and \(b=4\).

[STRUCTURAL HYPOTHESIS]

The individual subdivision lengths and their distribution matter jointly.

---

## C. Concentration versus balance

[DIRECTLY FROM DATA]

There is **no simple monotone concentration rule**.

At \(S=6\):

* highly concentrated \((6)\): \(b=3\);
* moderately concentrated \((4,2)\): \(b=3\);
* balanced \((2,2,2)\): \(b=4\);
* more spread-out \((2,2,1,1)\): \(b=3\);
* completely uniform over six branches \((1,1,1,1,1,1)\): \(b=3\).

At \(S=7\), both extremes already occur among the \(b=4\) cases:

$$
(7)
$$

and

$$
(2,2,2,1).
$$

[STRUCTURAL HYPOTHESIS]

The full partition shape appears more informative than a single scalar measure of "concentration."

---

## D. Number of branches with \(s_i\ge2\)

[DIRECTLY FROM DATA]

This statistic also does not uniquely determine \(b(T)\).

For example, at \(S=6\):

* \((2,2,2)\): three branches have \(s_i\ge2\), \(b=4\);
* \((4,1,1)\): one branch has \(s_i\ge2\), \(b=3\);
* \((3,2,1)\): two branches have \(s_i\ge2\), \(b=3\);
* \((2,2,1,1)\): two branches have \(s_i\ge2\), \(b=3\).

Thus the same number of branches meeting \(s_i\ge2\) can produce different burning numbers.

---

## E. Number of branches with \(s_i\ge3\)

[DIRECTLY FROM DATA]

Likewise this is not decisive.

At \(S=6\):

* \((6)\): one branch has \(s_i\ge3\), \(b=3\);
* \((4,1,1)\): one branch has \(s_i\ge3\), \(b=3\);
* \((3,1,1,1)\): one branch has \(s_i\ge3\), \(b=3\);
* \((3,3)\): two branches have \(s_i\ge3\), \(b=3\);
* \((2,2,2)\): zero branches have \(s_i\ge3\), yet \(b=4\).

This makes the \(S=6\) exception particularly clear.

[STRUCTURAL HYPOTHESIS]

The exceptional behavior at \(S=6\) is not naturally explained by merely counting "long" branches.

